      "problem": "Use the definition of the derivative to find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if  $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}+5x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The definition of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}+5x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4(x+h)^{2}+5(x+h)-(-4{x}^{2}+5x)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4({x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2})+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4{x}^{2}-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5h}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{h(-8x-4h+5)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-8x-4h+5) \\\\  & =-8x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
